According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are different for function $h$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}h(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\Big(f(x)h(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^-}f(x)=\lim_{x\to 0^+}f(x)=1}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^-}h(x)=3}
\\\\
&\goldD{\lim_{x\to 0^+}h(x)=0}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0}\Big(f(x)h(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a product of functions is the product of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^-}\Big(f(x)h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to 0^-}f(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to 0^-}h(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^+}\Big(f(x)h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to 0^+}f(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to 0^+}h(x)
\end{align}$
